# Erdős Problem #99 — AG47 propagation through the Type-II scalar and defect ledgers

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Repository base:** `d03ba81c`
**Target:** Target C-II canonical full-core Type-II branch
**Claim class:** proof-only propagation of AG47
**External input:** none
**Computation:** exact-rational sign certificate for the Section-171 endpoint

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The chain-cover refinement proves the proof-only atomic gap

\[
\eta_{grid}=0.00047.
\]

This note propagates that single improvement through the already-established correlated
stopped-train, Section-171 orbit/detour, chamber-interface, and residual-budget ledgers.
No new geometric theorem is introduced here.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
s_2=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
\rho=0.7390851332151606\ldots,
\]

where `rho=cos(rho)` is the canonical difference-body constant, and

\[
C_{146}={4\over9}(\pi+s_2).
\]

## 1. Correlated stopped-train split

The exact identity

\[
\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R-q
=2\left(T-{q\over2}-2\sqrt{R_4}\right)
\]

and AG47 give

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R-q\over D}\ge0.00094.
}
\tag{P47.1}
\]

Using `q/D>=2rho-o(1)`,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R\over D}
\ge2\rho+0.00094
=1.479110266430321\ldots .
}
\tag{P47.2}
\]

Hence at least one of

\[
\boxed{
S={\rho_{rec}\over2}
\ge(\rho/2+0.000235-o(1))D
=0.369777566607580\ldots D-o(D),
}
\tag{P47.3}
\]

or

\[
\boxed{
\sigma_R\ge(\rho+0.00047-o(1))D
=0.739555133215160\ldots D-o(D)
}
\tag{P47.4}
\]

must hold.

On the rhombic branch, the exact rationalization

\[
M_R={\sigma_R\over8}
\left(4\sqrt{R_4}+{\sigma_R\over2}\right)
\]

gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {M_R\over D^2}
\ge {\rho+0.00047\over8}
\left(4s_2+{\rho+0.00047\over2}\right)
=0.386327774562944\ldots .
}
\tag{P47.5}
\]

The exact post-endpoint scalar identity similarly improves to

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\mu_D
\ge\mu_{TC}+{\rho\over5}+{0.00047\over5}
=1.785473210824532\ldots .
}
\tag{P47.6}
\]

The direct correlated endpoint/interface floor becomes

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {J\over D}
\ge2\rho+4s_2-\pi+0.00094
=2.146768840296357\ldots .
}
\tag{P47.7}
\]

This is weaker than the Section-171 floor below, but is independent of the additional
orbit/detour scalar envelope.

## 2. Section-171 endpoint with `g=0.00047`

Audited Section 171 reduces every Type-II subsequential limit

\[
t=\lim{q-k\over D},
\qquad
\delta=\lim{b-P_F\over D}\ge0
\]

to

\[
F_t(\delta)=
10t-5\pi-5\delta
+\left(81(\pi+\delta)^2\delta\right)^{1/3}
-{8\Delta_2\over9t/2-2\pi-2\delta}
\ge0.
\tag{P47.8}
\]

With train gain `g`, the feasible detour interval is

\[
0\le\delta\le
{{9\over2}(t-C_{146})-g\over2}.
\tag{P47.9}
\]

Set

\[
g=0.00047,
\qquad
\varepsilon_{47}=0.0013855,
\qquad
t_{47}=C_{146}+\varepsilon_{47}.
\tag{P47.10}
\]

Then the right endpoint is

\[
\delta_{47}=0.002882375.
\tag{P47.11}
\]

The exact-rational certificate verifies:

1. `F_{t47}` is strictly increasing throughout the feasible detour interval;
2. `F_{t47}(delta47)<0` with a positive outward-rounding margin; and
3. Section 171's derivative in `t` is positive on the same domain.

Therefore no feasible Type-II limit has `t<=t47`, and

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}
\ge C_{146}+0.0013855
=1.820899037979444\ldots .
}
\tag{P47.12}
\]

A high-precision numerical root of the unchanged envelope is

\[
C_{146}+0.00138568661018\ldots,
\]

so the stated value retains a safe margin.  As with AG42, further decimal tuning of
this same scalar envelope is not a useful route.

## 3. Stronger mandatory chamber/interface layer

AG47 gives

\[
{T-q/2\over D}\ge2s_2+0.00047-o(1).
\]

Using the exact identity `J=2T-P_F` and (P47.12),

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {J\over D}
\ge j_{47}:=t_{47}+2(2s_2+0.00047)-\pi
=2.489497611845481\ldots .
}
\tag{P47.13}
\]

The exact chamber decomposition

\[
\Sigma_{ch}=J+a+L_{add}+R_F
\]
therefore gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\Sigma_{ch}\over D}
\ge2.489497611845481\ldots .
}
\tag{P47.14}
\]

Again, this is mandatory boundary/interface credit, not by itself repacking spare.

## 4. Exact H bootstrap and rhombus deficit

The exact relation

\[
H={J\over2}+(q-k)-{P_F\over2}
\]

with `P_F/D->pi` gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {H\over D}
\ge h_{47}:={j_{47}\over2}+t_{47}-{\pi\over2}
=1.494851517107288\ldots .
}
\tag{P47.15}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch}\over D}
\ge {\sqrt3\over4}h_{47}
=0.647289694350310\ldots,
}
\tag{P47.16}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\Delta_2D^2-R_4\over D}
\ge {h_{47}\over2}
=0.747425758553644\ldots .
}
\tag{P47.17}
\]

## 5. Corrected residual budget

The finite corrected residual cap remains

\[
R_F+\Sigma_{ext}
<{P_K\over2}+P_F-q-{\delta_K+E_4\over a_0}.
\]

Using `q>=q-k`, `P_K,P_F<=pi D`, and (P47.12) gives the slightly sharper explicit
first-order ceiling

\[
\boxed{
\limsup {R_F+\Sigma_{ext}\over D}
\le {3\pi\over2}-t_{47}
=2.891489942405245\ldots .
}
\tag{P47.18}
\]

The gain is numerically small, but it propagates in the correct direction: AG47 raises
the mandatory interface floor while lowering the genuine residual budget ceiling.

## 6. Research-state effect

AG47 materially strengthens the current full-core split but still does not close C-II.
The two hard mechanisms are now quantitatively:

```text
singleton branch:
    at least 0.3697775666 D singleton stopped trains;

rhombic branch:
    sigma_R >= 0.7395551332 D,
    M_R >= 0.3863277746 D^2.
```

The Section-171 scalar floor is effectively saturated relative to its current analytic
envelope.  Further progress should attack one of those two geometric mechanisms, not
continue tuning the scalar endpoint.

**Disposition:** proof-only AG47 propagation. C-II remains open.
